      title: "Barnabas and the Covenant",
      body: `## "It is ours"

The central argument of Barnabas is stark. In chapter 4, he warns his readers: "Do not be like some people, piling up your sins and saying that the covenant is both theirs and ours. It is ours; but they lost it completely in this way, when Moses had just received it." He refers to the story of the golden calf (Exodus 32). When Moses came down from Mount Sinai with the tablets of the covenant and saw the people worshipping the calf, he threw down the tablets and broke them. For Barnabas, this meant that Israel lost the covenant at the very moment it was given, "so that the covenant of the beloved Jesus might be sealed in our hearts."

This is a more radical claim than anything we have met in the other Apostolic Fathers, or in Paul. Paul had argued, in his letter to the Romans, that God had not rejected his people Israel, that "the gifts and the calling of God are irrevocable" (Romans 11:29), and that "all Israel will be saved" (Romans 11:26). For Barnabas, Israel never had the covenant at all. The Jews' whole history of worship and obedience was a mistake, the result of a literal misreading of a spiritual revelation. "An evil angel," he says of circumcision, "deceived them."

## Sabbath, Temple, and sacrifice

Barnabas applies this argument to all the institutions of Israel. God did not want **sacrifices**; he said through the prophets, "What is the multitude of your sacrifices to me?" (Isaiah 1:11). He did not want physical **circumcision**, but the circumcision of the heart and ears. He did not want the **Sabbath** as the Jews kept it. The Sabbath of Genesis, when God rested on the seventh day after six days of creation, means, Barnabas argues, that the world will last six thousand years—since "a day with the Lord is as a thousand years" (Psalm 90:4)—and will then come to its rest. "Your present Sabbaths are not acceptable to me," God says through Isaiah, but "the Sabbath I have made, in which, having given rest to all things, I will make the beginning of an eighth day, that is, the beginning of another world. This is why we also keep the **eighth day** with gladness, on which Jesus also rose from the dead." Sunday, the "eighth day," is the Christian day.

The **Temple**, too, was a mistake. The Jews, "like the pagans," consecrated God in a building; but the true temple of God is the human heart in which God dwells. Its destruction was God's judgment, and its rebuilding by the Romans would only confirm it.

## Why so harsh?

Why did Barnabas argue so harshly? Scholars have offered several explanations. Some think he wrote for Gentile Christians who were attracted to Judaism, and who were tempted to say that "the covenant is both theirs and ours." If Christians and Jews shared the covenant, why not keep the Sabbath and the food laws, and join in the hopes of the Jewish community for the rebuilding of the Temple? Barnabas wanted to cut off this possibility by denying that Judaism had ever been valid. The British scholar **James Carleton Paget**, in a major study of the letter, has suggested that Barnabas wrote in a situation of real rivalry with a confident Jewish community, perhaps at a time of heightened Jewish hopes for the restoration of Jerusalem in the years before the Bar Kokhba revolt. Others see the letter as an internal Christian argument about how to read Scripture, with "the Jews" functioning mainly as a symbol of wrong reading.

## Supersessionism

Barnabas represents an extreme form of what theologians call **supersessionism**: the belief that the Church has replaced Israel as the people of God. Most later Christian theologians did not follow him all the way. They held that the old covenant had been valid in its time, given by God to Israel to prepare for Christ, and then fulfilled and superseded by the new. **Marcion**, at the opposite extreme, rejected the Jewish Scriptures entirely, as the work of a lesser god. The mainstream church rejected both positions: against Marcion, it insisted that the Scriptures of Israel were the word of the one true God; against Barnabas, most theologians allowed that Israel's covenant had been real. But the idea that the Church had replaced Israel, and that the Jews had misunderstood their own Scriptures, became a central and destructive theme of Christian thought about Judaism for many centuries.

Since the Second World War, many Christian churches have rejected supersessionism and returned to Paul's insistence that God's covenant with Israel has not been revoked. Reading Barnabas today, we encounter both an early Christian passion for finding Christ in the Scriptures and the beginnings of an attitude whose consequences would be grave.

## The Two Ways again

The letter ends, surprisingly, with a version of the **Two Ways**, which we met in the Didache. Here they are the way of light and the way of darkness, presided over by angels of God and angels of Satan. The moral teaching is very close to that of the Didache, including the commandment not to kill a child by abortion or after birth. The parallel suggests that both documents drew on a common source, probably the Jewish moral teaching we discussed in the first lesson. It is a reminder that even a writer as hostile to Judaism as Barnabas drew, without knowing it or acknowledging it, on Jewish tradition.

## Barnabas's legacy

The Epistle of Barnabas was treasured in Alexandria and beyond for its spiritual interpretations of Scripture, and it shaped the way Christians read the Old Testament. The typology of Moses's outstretched arms as the cross, and of the Sunday as the "eighth day," became part of the common Christian heritage. But its harsh view of Israel's covenant, its fanciful natural history, and its arbitrary allegories kept it out of the canon, and modern readers have found it the least attractive of the Apostolic Fathers. It shows us the early Church in the midst of a struggle to define itself against Judaism, a struggle whose costs would be paid for centuries.

> **Pause and consider.** Barnabas insists that "the covenant is ours," not both theirs and ours. Why might some Christians of his time have believed that the covenant was shared? What was at stake in denying it?`,
