      title: "The Shepherd of Hermas: Visions in Rome",
      body: `## A freedman's story

The **Shepherd of Hermas** is the longest of the Apostolic Fathers—longer than any book of the New Testament—and in the early Church it was one of the most popular. It begins with a story that sounds like the opening of a Greek romance:

"The man who raised me sold me to a certain Rhoda in Rome. After many years, I met her again and began to love her as a sister. Some time later, I saw her bathing in the river Tiber, and I gave her my hand and helped her out of the river. When I saw her beauty, I thought in my heart, 'How happy I would be if I had a wife of such beauty and character.' This was my only thought, and nothing more."

Hermas, then, was a former slave, raised and sold in Rome, who had been freed and become a businessman of modest means, with a wife and children. Some time later, he tells us, he was walking and fell asleep, and in a vision Rhoda appeared to him from heaven and accused him of sinning against her in his heart. "Not in so many words," she said, "but the desire for evil came into your heart." Hermas was astonished and terrified. Could even a thought be a sin? If God recorded even this against him, how could he be saved?

Whether the story is autobiography or literary invention is debated. It is told with a vividness and self-deprecation that are unusual in early Christian literature. Hermas portrays himself as an anxious, rather slow-witted, well-meaning man, whose children have turned out badly and whose wife talks too much, and who is repeatedly scolded by the heavenly beings who instruct him for his foolish questions.

## The old woman growing young

In his next vision, Hermas sees an old woman in shining garments, seated on a great white chair, reading from a book. She tells him that God is angry with him, not because of Rhoda, but because of his family's sins, and calls him to repentance. Later, in another vision, he asks who she is. He thinks she is the Sibyl, the pagan prophetess. No, he is told: she is **the Church**. "Why is she old?" "Because she was created before all things; that is why she is old, and for her sake the world was made."

In each successive vision, the woman appears younger. First she is old and seated in a chair; then she stands, with a younger face; finally she is young and beautiful, "as if she had come from a bridal chamber." The interpretation is given: the Church appeared old because the faith of the believers was old and weak; as they repented and were renewed, she became young again. The image combines the ancient Jewish and Christian idea that the people of God was created before the world with a vivid picture of spiritual renewal.

## The tower

In the third vision, the woman shows Hermas a great **tower** being built upon the waters by six young men, with stones brought by thousands of others. Some stones fit perfectly; some are thrown away; some are set aside near the tower but not used; some are cracked or too round to fit. The tower is the Church; the water is baptism; the stones are believers. The square, white stones that fit perfectly are the apostles, bishops, teachers, and deacons who have lived in holiness, and the martyrs. The rejected stones are sinners. Those set aside near the tower can still be fitted in if they repent quickly, before the tower is finished—that is, before the end comes. The image of the Church as a building rising toward completion, with each believer as a stone, recurs throughout the book, developed at great length in its ninth part.

## The Shepherd

After the visions, a new figure appears: a man "glorious in appearance, dressed like a shepherd, with a white goatskin wrapped around him, a bag on his shoulders, and a staff in his hand." He is the **angel of repentance**, sent to live with Hermas and teach him. From him the book takes its name. He gives Hermas twelve **Mandates**, or commandments, and ten **Similitudes**, or parables, which make up the greater part of the book. The Mandates teach faith, simplicity, truthfulness, chastity, patience, discernment of spirits, the fear of God, self-control, and freedom from doubt and grief. The first Mandate is one of the earliest Christian statements of creation from nothing: "First of all, believe that God is one, who created all things and set them in order, and made all things to exist out of what did not exist."

## Rich and poor

Hermas's world is the world of ordinary Christians in Rome, and many of his concerns are practical. He worries about Christians who are absorbed in business, "entangled in their affairs and wealth and friendships with pagans," and who neglect their faith. One of the most charming parables compares rich and poor Christians to an **elm and a vine**. The elm bears no fruit, but the vine, when it climbs on the elm, bears fruit abundantly; without the elm, the vine lies on the ground and its fruit rots. So the rich, who are poor in prayer because of their wealth, should support the poor, who are rich in prayer; and the poor should pray for the rich. Each needs the other, and both together bear fruit.

The American scholar **Carolyn Osiek**, in her commentary on the Shepherd, has shown how much it reveals about the social life of Roman Christians: their business dealings, their anxieties about wealth, their family troubles, their relations with pagan neighbors, and their struggles to live the Christian life in a city full of temptation. It is a book written not for theologians but for ordinary believers, and it found a vast readership among them.

> **Pause and consider.** Hermas portrays the Church as an old woman who grows younger as believers repent. What does this image suggest about the relationship between the Church and its members?`,
